1. Finding what to learn
Open 10 junior Linux, cloud or sysadmin job posts (LinkedIn, Indeed) in the country you want.
Write down the tools that appear in most of them. If something appears in 7 of 10, learn it. If it appears once, skip it.
Compare your list with the checklist and add anything important that's missing. roadmap.sh has a free DevOps map if you want a second view.
2. How to learn
Use the same loop for every topic:
Read or watch a little (30 to 60 minutes at most).
Do it yourself in the terminal, without copying line by line.
Break it on purpose, then fix it.
Write 5 lines about what you did in a README on GitHub.
Most of your time should go on steps 2 and 3, not on watching videos. Use the official docs and free sources like Linux Journey, freeCodeCamp and the Docker and AWS docs. Use me as a tutor: paste your errors and ask "why did this happen?" instead of asking for finished answers.
3. How the checklist works
It's a to-do list. Each line is one skill.
Go from top to bottom. Phase 1 comes before Phase 2.
Tick a line only when you can do it from scratch, without a guide, and explain it in your own words.
If you can't, stay on that line. Moving on too early is the most common reason people get stuck later.